WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.933
Build capacity nearly doubled.

00:00:01.933 --> 00:00:03.400
Accepted outcomes did not.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.600
This is Leverage Lab.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.766
It is a small simulator that shows why.

00:00:07.933 --> 00:00:11.433
Work moves through six stages, from shape to adopt.

00:00:11.433 --> 00:00:15.333
Work counts only when people use it and someone owns it.

00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:19.533
Each stage can pass a limited amount of good work each week.

00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:21.666
Build can pass about 19 items.

00:00:21.666 --> 00:00:23.366
Verify can pass about 5.

00:00:23.366 --> 00:00:23.500
7.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.666
So Verify sets the pace for the whole system.

00:00:27.866 --> 00:00:29.533
But you cannot see that.

00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:34.300
Telemetry shows 95 percent of Build, and only 32 percent of Verify.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.866
Build has the biggest queue and the most activity.

00:00:37.866 --> 00:00:42.133
So the evidence points at Build, with 71 percent confidence.

00:00:42.133 --> 00:00:45.966
The loudest stage is not the stage that limits output.

00:00:47.166 --> 00:00:49.100
Suppose you trust that evidence.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.533
Every cycle, you add more agents and a delivery partner.

00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:56.033
Over four six-week cycles, accepted outcomes fall from 5.

00:00:56.033 --> 00:00:56.466
2 to 1.

00:00:56.466 --> 00:00:57.133
3 per week.

00:00:57.133 --> 00:00:59.866
Work in progress grows from 64 items to 148.

00:00:59.866 --> 00:01:02.100
More building made the system worse.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.633
Now spend at the real constraint.

00:01:05.633 --> 00:01:09.600
In cycle one, automate verification and limit new work.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:10.933
Output rises to 6.

00:01:10.933 --> 00:01:14.200
5 per week, and the constraint moves to Adopt.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.966
In cycle two, fund adoption and make handoffs clear.

00:01:17.966 --> 00:01:21.600
Output reaches 10 per week, as the backlog drains.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.333
Then output falls back to 4.

00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:26.466
7 per week, even if you change nothing.

00:01:26.466 --> 00:01:28.266
The 10 was the backlog draining.

00:01:28.266 --> 00:01:31.833
Now the stages can pass about 9 items a week, but only 5 arrive.

00:01:31.833 --> 00:01:33.633
The limit has left the pipeline.

00:01:33.633 --> 00:01:35.700
It is now how much new work comes in.

00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:38.166
The constraint moves.

00:01:38.166 --> 00:01:40.133
Find it again after every change.

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:41.766
Try it yourself in the lab.
